 "background": [
  {
   "text": "Dual NAV IRS failures cause the loss of AP, FD and A/THR, and the flight controls revert to ALTN law.",
   "applies_to": [
    "all"
   ],
   "refs": [
    {
     "book": "FCTM",
     "code": "PR-AEP-NAV",
     "page": "P 1/20",
     "pdf": 478
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "(3) in the table: after the faulty IR is set to OFF and ELAC 1 and 2 are reset.",
   "applies_to": [
    "all"
   ],
   "refs": [
    {
     "book": "FCOM",
     "code": "DSC-27-20-20",
     "page": "P 3/20",
     "pdf": 1614
    },
    {
     "book": "FCOM",
     "code": "DSC-27-20-20",
     "page": "P 5/20",
     "pdf": 1616
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "There is no procedure for IRS 1 + 2 + 3 failure; the ECAM STATUS shows the approach procedure and INOP systems, and the standby instruments are the only attitude, altitude, speed and heading references.",
   "applies_to": [
    "all"
   ],
   "refs": [
    {
     "book": "FCTM",
     "code": "PR-AEP-NAV",
     "page": "P 1/20",
     "pdf": 478
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "ATT HDG selector: CAPT 3 — IR 3 replaces IR 1; F/O 3 — IR 3 replaces IR 2.",
   "applies_to": [
    "all"
   ],
   "refs": [
    {
     "book": "FCOM",
     "code": "DSC-34-NAV-10-20",
     "page": "P 3/6",
     "pdf": 2102
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "IR FAULT light flashing: attitude and heading may be recovered in ATT mode. Steady: the IR is lost.",
   "applies_to": [
    "all"
   ],
   "refs": [
    {
     "book": "FCOM",
     "code": "DSC-34-NAV-10-20",
     "page": "P 2/6",
     "pdf": 2101
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "In ATT mode the IR gives only attitude and heading (heading entered on the MCDU, reset about every 10 min). Once an IR is set from NAV to ATT or OFF in flight, NAV mode is lost for the rest of the flight; it is recovered only on ground by a complete alignment.",
   "applies_to": [
    "all"
   ],
   "refs": [
    {
     "book": "FCOM",
     "code": "DSC-34-NAV-10-20",
     "page": "P 2/6",
     "pdf": 2101
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "technique": [
  {
   "text": "The PF usually remains PF, unless the captain re-allocates tasks or a failure impacts the PF's FLY task (e.g. loss of attitude on the PF side).",
   "applies_to": [
    "all"
   ],
   "refs": [
    {
     "book": "FCTM",
     "code": "AOP-30-30",
     "page": "P 1/6",
     "pdf": 82
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "To take control, the pilot clearly announces \"I have control\" and presses and holds the sidestick pb.",
   "applies_to": [
    "all"
   ],
   "refs": [
    {
     "book": "FCTM",
     "code": "AOP-10-30-20",
     "page": "P 1/2",
     "pdf": 62
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "text": "Reconfiguration law: at high altitude descend to increase the buffet margin (about 4 000 ft below REC MAX ALT); at high speed maneuver with care using small inputs.",
   "applies_to": [
    "all"
   ],
   "refs": [
    {
     "book": "FCTM",
     "code": "AOP-10-30-20",
     "page": "P 1/2",
     "pdf": 62
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
